   "history": "Misir Wat is the most important vegetarian dish in Ethiopian cuisine — during the 200+ annual fasting days observed by Ethiopia's Orthodox Christian majority, when meat and dairy are prohibited, Misir Wat is the primary protein and the most beloved dish on the fasting table. Made from red lentils cooked in a berbere sauce built on dry-caramelised onions and oil (no kibbeh on fasting days), it is simultaneously the simplest and most satisfying of Ethiopian dishes. The key technique is identical to Key Wat: dry-caramelise the onions first, bloom the berbere in oil, then cook the lentils until they dissolve into the sauce."
