   "history": "Dabo Kolo (literally 'hard bread') is one of Ethiopia's most ancient snacks — small, crunchy pieces of spiced bread that are baked until completely hard and dry. They have the shelf life of a biscuit without any processing and are the traditional accompaniment to Ethiopian coffee ceremonies, passed around in baskets while coffee is served. They come in sweet versions (with honey and cinnamon), savoury-spiced (with berbere), and plain. Sold in every market, every school, and at every bus stop."
