  "heritage": {
   "origin": "Kaffa region — southwestern Ethiopia, the birthplace of Coffea arabica",
   "history": "The Kaffa region (from which the word 'coffee' is etymologically derived) in southwestern Ethiopia is the biological homeland of Coffea arabica — wild coffee grows here in the montane forest as it has for millions of years. The Kaffa people have been using coffee longer than anyone else on earth — they chew the whole coffee berry mixed with butter and salt (chewing the whole fruit rather than brewing it) and have a distinctly different coffee preparation tradition from the rest of Ethiopia. Kaffa forest coffee is the original — gathered from wild trees in the forest, prepared in ways that predate the coffee ceremony."
  },
