   "origin": "Kenyan Swahili Coast — Mombasa, Lamu, and the Indian Ocean coastline",
   "history": "Samaki wa Kupaka is one of the great dishes of Swahili coastal cuisine, born from centuries of Indian Ocean trade that brought Arab, Persian, and Indian culinary traditions to East Africa. The marriage of grilled fish with coconut milk sauce — enriched with turmeric, coriander, and chilli — reflects the layered cultural history of Kenya's coast, where spice routes shaped not just commerce but daily life and the food people cook. In coastal homes from Mombasa to Lamu, this dish is prepared for family meals, seaside gatherings, and celebrations.",
