   "origin": "Eastern and Central Kenya — where green grams have been grown for generations",
   "history": "Green grams — known as ndengu in Swahili — have been cultivated in East Africa for centuries, arriving through Indian Ocean trade routes that brought legumes and spices from the Indian subcontinent. In Kenya, ndengu became especially popular in the drier Eastern and Central regions where they thrive in the climate and soil. Affordable, quick to cook without soaking, and nutritionally dense, ndengu stew became a staple of everyday Kenyan family cooking.",
